From 62413c68f566641dc407db4ad49ebaf6b60bcd2b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: exeroom536 <352890649+exeroom536@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 11:18:41 +0500
Subject: [PATCH 33/35] super-amd: grow volumes in place, and a RAID0 into a
 RAID10

The AMD driver (rcadm -T, RAIDXpert2) changes a volume by a migration:
the volume's LD and the LD it becomes, under a transform log.  Two of
them md runs as they are, on the same layout:

- mdadm --grow <volume> --size=<KiB>|max of a RAID1, RAID10 or RAID5:
  every member grows in place, its data where they are, up to the next
  extent of any volume or the end of its disk (for more the AMD driver
  moves the volume elsewhere, which md cannot), in whole MiB, never
  smaller.  mdadm records the migration as rcadm -T -s does, md resyncs
  the new part from the old size, and mdmon logs its progress and ends
  it in the "100 %" packet and the final one, as the AMD driver does;
- mdadm --grow <RAID0 of N> --level=10 [--raid-devices=2N]: the RAID10
  N x 2 rcadm -T -r10 makes of it - the members stay where they are, a
  mirror of each goes onto a spare of the container (N of them, laid out
  as for a rebuild).  md takes the RAID0 over as raid10 near-2, mdmon
  gives it the mirrors all at once and records the copy; a mirror md
  fails on the way is recorded failed, and the migration goes on.  mdmon
  is started for it if it does not run for the container (RAID0 and
  Volume volumes only).

Either side goes on with the other's migration from its log: md with
one the AMD driver started (-E shows it; the volume is assembled as it
is to be), the AMD driver with one md was running.  A migration starts
only as the AMD driver starts one: on a normal volume, every member in
service and here, no other transform under way; no rebuild starts while
the capacity grows.  If Grow.c cannot set md to the new size, the
migration is dropped, as the AMD driver drops one the user cancels.  A
migration that loses a member on the way waits for the volume to be
whole again (the AMD driver would finish it degraded).

Checked with the AMD Linux driver (rcraid 9.3.3) in a QEMU guest: the
records of a RAID1 grown and of a RAID0 made a RAID10 are those it
writes, field for field (but the capacity, the generations, TaskPriority
and the element handles); it finishes our migrations of RAID1, RAID5,
RAID10 and RAID0 -> RAID10, interrupted or not, and md finishes its -
the data the same.  Not tried with the Windows driver nor the BIOS.

Written by Claude Opus 5.5, an AI model by Anthropic.
